# Reference run of horizon_nogo_numerics.py
# python 3.12.3 | numpy 2.4.4 | scipy 1.17.1 | matplotlib 3.10.8 | Linux x86_64


(i) Shooting outward from a regular horizon (radial, horizon-homogeneous).
    If a regular layer existed, some phi_h would reach a vacuum.
    k     radii            max |phi(600)| over 123 shots
   +1    [5.0, 6.0, 12.0]  3.57e-03
   +0    [5.0, 6.0, 12.0]  3.67e-03
   -1    [5.0, 6.0, 12.0]  3.74e-03
    every trajectory relaxes toward phi = 0; none approaches +-v.
    Scope: profiles depending on r alone.  Static walls with angular
    dependence lie outside this ansatz.

(ii) Hypothesis of the no-go: Delta' > 0 on the exterior.
     k     r_h     min Delta'(r) on [r_h, 400]
    +1     5.0         28.4375
    +1     6.0         46.5000
    +1    12.0        336.0000
    +1    32.0       6176.0000
    +0     5.0         23.4375
    +0     6.0         40.5000
    +0    12.0        324.0000
    +0    32.0       6144.0000
    -1     5.0         18.4375
    -1     6.0         34.5000
    -1    12.0        312.0000
    -1    32.0       6112.0000

(iii) The stretched-horizon offset is a defining scale.
      On the finite numerical interval [a,b] the exact balance is
      Delta(a) phi'(a)^2/2 = Delta(b) phi'(b)^2/2 + a^2 V(0)
                              + int_a^b [Delta'(phi')^2/2 + 2rV].
   eps        lhs         rhs        rel.diff    phi'(a)   lower bound   ratio      E
    1e-01     39.1426     39.1426    1.0e-10       1.63         0.86    1.90    27.2747
    3e-02     44.6252     44.6252    1.2e-09       3.40         1.57    2.16    14.6749
    1e-02     63.1063     63.1063    1.0e-08       7.14         2.72    2.62     9.6839
    3e-03    108.8309    108.8309    1.2e-07      17.23         4.97    3.47     6.7678
    1e-03    199.3806    199.3808    1.0e-06      40.47         8.61    4.70     5.1897
    3e-04    422.5761    422.5810    1.2e-05     107.64        15.71    6.85     4.0769
    1e-04    891.6064    891.6992    1.0e-04     270.87        27.22    9.95     3.3863
      the rigorous lower bound grows as eps^{-1/2}; the computed slope
      grows even faster over this range, so no exact power law is claimed.
      The energy decreases monotonically over this range.  What is
      established is that the slope diverges, so no nontrivial
      horizon-regular configuration satisfying the pinned datum
      survives eps -> 0.  No claim is made that the energy lacks a
      limiting value; the data are equally consistent with a
      gradual collapse onto the vacuum.
